e("  { v: '9.47', t: 'Harder climbs', n: [", "  { v: '9.48', t: 'Owner mode', n: [\n    'New: Owner mode (Mode 5 on the Home screen). Buy an NFL club or start an expansion club, and run it from the owner\\'s suite. It uses one of your five AD save slots.',\n    'Getting a club: four clubs are for sale, each with a story. Put together the money from your own fortune, club debt (up to the league limit) and minority partners; the controlling owner must put in at least 30% of the equity. Outbid the other groups, then win at least 24 of the owners\\' votes.',\n    'Expansion: pick a city (market, local wealth, football passion, stadium), choose a conference and division, make up a name, colors and a crest, pay the fee, and stock the roster in an expansion draft against the other new club. New clubs get 75% of the national money for three seasons and often start in a rented stadium.',\n    'Three scenario starts with goals: the worst club in the smallest market, football\\'s new frontier (an expansion city that never had the NFL), and the family business (51% of a club, an old stadium and a lot of debt). A former player or GM from another career on this device can buy in as a 10% partner and take control after a season.',\n    'The business: a full profit-and-loss statement (national money, tickets, suites, concessions, parking, sponsors, naming rights, merchandise, stadium events, revenue sharing; payroll, staff, football budgets, stadium costs, interest). Set ticket and concession prices, decide how much profit to pay out, borrow or pay down debt, and watch the club\\'s value. Run out of money and the league forces a sale.',\n    'The stadium: seven improvement projects, naming-rights offers, and a new stadium (size, open air, dome or retractable roof) financed with public money, seat licenses, a league loan and your own money. The city may say yes, send it to the voters, or turn you down. Threaten to leave, or ask the owners to approve a move to a new city.',\n    'Fans and brand: fan mood, brand, city relations, season tickets and a waiting list, community programs, new uniforms (fans may hate them), throwback games, and moments that ask for the owner\\'s answer.',\n    'The football side: hire a GM or be your own GM (switch each offseason). Hire and fire the GM and head coach, set a direction (win now, balanced, rebuild) and five football budgets. Orders to your GM (release, trade, re-sign, extend, free-agent and draft targets, blocking his deadline deal) count as meddling, and good candidates turn down meddling owners.',\n    'League politics: an owners\\' meeting every spring with rule changes that really change the league, revenue sharing, other clubs\\' sales and moves, your own proposal, Super Bowl and draft hosting bids, favors you can call in, labor talks that can end in a lockout, TV deals, and a relationship with the commissioner (prime-time games, or fines).',\n    'Legacy: points for winning, titles, a growing club, a new stadium and community work; the Hall of Fame for owners; sell the club, hand it to an heir and keep playing, or retire.',\n    'Balance (NFL, Updated balance): your own club no longer wins far more than its roster should. Game plans used to help only your side; they count for less now, since the other club game-plans too. Locker-room morale moves strength half as much, and your games now play to their own pregame odds. Tested on autopilot, a GM\\'s club averaged 12.8 wins and won the Super Bowl a third of the time; now it averages about 8.4 wins, in line with its roster. This also applies to NFL general managers and NFL coaches. Classic saves are unchanged.',\n    'Fixed: in a league with more or fewer than 32 clubs (commissioner expansion), some clubs got almost all home games one season and none the next. Home and away games are balanced now.',\n  ] },\n" + "  { v: '9.47', t: 'Harder climbs', n: [")

